Z||000000||gtsonly||07-13-2000||01:53 AM||||talking with gokart tonight, are there any means or ways for you to polish out scratches in the side windows? rumor may have me believe that the window will shatter if this is attempted, because of the composition of the glass. big word, eh? i cant see where a creme window polish would have that result but i turn to you all for info. please help!!!!!<P>      chris hatch||||reg||5
Z||000001||tgowens||07-13-2000||03:39 AM||||Chris, Meguire's has a clear plastic polish and a plastic "repair" - it helps <I>some</I> but if yours is <I>really</I> heat cracked it won't get it out. if they are only light surface scratches it works fine. -- I ended up replacing mine with reproduction windows they were so badly cracked.<P>------------------<BR>     '87 Fiero GT<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.tgowens.com/t_g_owens.gif">       <BR><A HREF="http://www.tgowens.com/fiero" TARGET=_blank>www.tgowens.com/fiero</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||RossT||07-13-2000||03:55 AM||||I just purchase a glass polish from <A HREF="http://www.zainobros.com" TARGET=_blank>www.zainobros.com</A>  that is suppose to remove small scratches and etched in water spots. It just came in the mail yesterday, so I will let you know how it works.||||reg||1
Z||000003||gtsonly||07-13-2000||05:57 AM||||i dont want to confuse anyone or myself for that matter. when i said side windows i meant the driver and passenger windows where it is common to get scratches from worn out dew wipes. i have faint scratches and wanted to try to polish them out but was told against it for fear of shattering the glass||||reg||1
Z||000004||RossT||07-13-2000||06:17 AM||||The product that I have is for GLASS. It can be used inside and outside. Can be used with or without a machine.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||07-13-2000||07:20 AM||||I was told by a glass man that if you can catch your fingernail in the scratch it's too deep to polish out. FWIW||||reg||1
Z||000006||RossT||09-25-2000||11:27 PM||||UPDATE!!!  UPDATE!!!<P>I finally had a chance to use the glass hand polish that I bought last summer. The problem that I was having was with the inside of my drivers side glass. Some kind of chemical or acid was etched into to the glass. And to make matters worse, the window was rolled up and down so the etching was smeared by the dew strips.  I tried everything to get the stain off.  Windex, spray on glass cleaner, vinagar, WD40, razor blade, clay bar.. you name it I tried it.  I took the GT to a glass shop and the guy took one look at it and said the window would have to be replaced for about $250.<P>The product that I used was from <A HREF="http://www.zainobros.com" TARGET=_blank>www.zainobros.com</A>  I used a damp cotton applicator and gently rubbed the product into the interior of the glass.  After two applications all the smear marks were GONE.  Looks like new glass!! Yes I was amazed.<P>The true test came when I tried on my truck topper tinted windows.  The windows had severe water spots for over 7 years and lots of scratches.  The product took out all the water spots and most of the fine scratches.  It did not take out the deeper scrateches.  But a much inprovement.<P>Ross||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||09-26-2000||05:33 AM||||VERY minor scratches can be rubbed or buffed out, but it does make a little distortion to looking thru it so dont do it to windshield. On the plastic windows, I have had great luck even with deep scratches. I wet sand the plastic with #1000 grit and use 3M superduty rubbing compound then Final Finish. Run buffer at slow speed with very little pressure. They turn out looking like new. Also works for the sun crazing on them.Ive also done this on large picture windows in some vans.||||reg||1
